      passage: "ĐOẠN A:\nMany learners finish a lesson by reading their notes again. This can feel productive because the page becomes familiar. However, familiarity is not the same as memory. A sentence may look obvious while it is open in front of us, but disappear when the book is closed. For this reason, rereading alone can give learners an inaccurate sense of what they can actually recall.\n\nĐOẠN B:\nAn English teacher named Elena introduced a ten-minute review at the end of each class. Students first closed their books and wrote down three key points from the lesson, together with one question they still had. They then compared their notes with a partner. Only after this exchange did they reopen the lesson and check what was missing, inaccurate or unclear. The same material returned briefly three days later and again at the end of the week.\n\nĐOẠN C:\nThe activity was not used to calculate marks. Its purpose was to make gaps visible while the lesson was still recent. Some students initially felt uncomfortable because they remembered less than expected. Elena explained that this difficulty was useful information rather than proof of failure. Over time, the class became faster at recalling main ideas and more specific when asking for help. The routine did not remove the need for longer study, but it gave that study a clearer direction.",
