      passage: "PART 1 (18.2):\nWhat do you usually do in the evening?\nI usually have dinner with my family and then spend some time preparing for the next day. If I'm not too tired, I study English for about twenty minutes. I prefer short evening sessions because they are easier to maintain.\n\nPART 2 (18.3):\nDescribe a useful piece of advice you received.\nA useful piece of advice I received was to make my study plan smaller, not more ambitious. It came from one of my teachers when I was trying to restart English after a long break. At that time, I kept creating large weekly schedules, but I rarely completed them, so I felt guilty and usually stopped after a few days.\nMy teacher told me to choose one task that I could still do on a busy or tiring day. I decided to record one short answer and review five phrases each evening. The routine looked almost too easy, but that was exactly why I could maintain it. After a few weeks, I had a collection of recordings and could hear that my answers were becoming clearer. The advice was useful because it changed the way I measured progress. Instead of asking whether I had studied for several hours, I asked whether I had returned to English that day.\n\nPART 3 (18.4):\nWhy do young people sometimes ignore useful advice?\nOne reason is that advice can feel too general or controlling, especially when it comes from someone who does not understand the young person's situation. For example, telling a student to 'work harder' may not help if the real problem is poor time management or anxiety. However, young people are usually more open to advice when it is specific and when they are allowed to make the final decision themselves.",
