      passage: "TASK 1 (19.2):\nThe table shows average daily phone use, in minutes, for four purposes among a group of students.\n\nBảng số liệu (Mục đích | 2024 | 2026):\nMessaging | 70 | 65\nWatching videos | 50 | 75\nStudying | 20 | 40\nGaming | 45 | 35\n\nCác thay đổi cần nhận ra: Messaging giảm nhẹ; Watching videos tăng mạnh và trở thành mục có thời lượng cao nhất; Studying tăng gấp đôi; Gaming giảm.\n\nBài viết mẫu:\nThe table compares the average number of minutes students spent on four phone activities per day in 2024 and 2026.\nOverall, video viewing and study time increased, whereas messaging and gaming declined. Watching videos replaced messaging as the most time-consuming activity by 2026.\nIn 2024, messaging ranked first at 70 minutes per day, followed by video viewing at 50 minutes. Two years later, the former had fallen slightly to 65 minutes, while the latter had climbed to 75 and taken the lead.\nStudy time doubled from 20 to 40 minutes. By contrast, gaming decreased by 10 minutes, from 45 to 35.\n\nTASK 2 (19.3):\nSome students prefer studying alone, while others learn better in groups. Which approach is more suitable for beginners?\n\nĐoạn mẫu:\nFor many beginners, studying with a small and supportive group can be more effective than working entirely alone. At an early stage, learners often cannot identify their own grammar or pronunciation errors, so feedback from a teacher or peer can prevent the same mistakes from becoming habits. For example, a student may repeatedly say 'I very like it' without realising that the natural phrase is 'I really like it'. A partner can notice and repair this immediately. Group study is not automatically useful, however; the group needs a clear task and enough speaking time for every member. Under these conditions, beginners receive support without becoming passive.",
